  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Comparisons",
    tags: [
      "muscle milk vs premier protein",
      "premier protein vs muscle milk",
      "muscle milk pro vs premier protein",
      "which is better muscle milk or premier protein",
      "muscle milk vs premier protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Protein Drink Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-drink/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Head-to-Head"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Muscle Milk vs Premier Protein</Fragment>
      The two biggest bottled protein shakes in America, compared label to label after Muscle Milk's 2026 reformulation: protein, sugar, sweeteners, lactose and what each shake really costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-changed">Muscle Milk's 2026 Change</a>
      <a href="#protein">Which Has More Protein?</a>
      <a href="#calories-sugar">Calories, Sugar and Fat</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners</a>
      <a href="#lactose">Lactose</a>
      <a href="#price">Price per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#powders">The Powders</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy Which</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Premier Protein is the leaner shake, with 30 g of protein, 1 g of sugar and 160 calories per carton, while the reformulated Muscle Milk is cheaper per gram of protein and is now sweetened with stevia instead of sucralose. Which one wins depends on whether you care more about sugar, sweeteners or price. Below we compare every like-for-like pair, shakes and powders.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Muscle Milk vs Premier Protein: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein is the better shake for low sugar and low carbs, and Muscle Milk is the better shake for price and for avoiding artificial sweeteners. A Premier carton gives 30 g of protein, 160 calories and 1 g of sugar, per the label at the time of writing. The reformulated 2026 Muscle Milk shake gives 26 g of protein, 160 calories and 3 g of sugar, and the Muscle Milk Pro carton gives 33 g of protein for about 200 calories. At the Amazon prices we saw, Muscle Milk cost about $1.68 per 25 g of protein against Premier's $1.86.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per carton (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Muscle Milk 26 g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Muscle Milk Pro 33 g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Premier 30 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 fl oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 fl oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 oz (11.5 oz bottle also sold)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">190-200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat / carbs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g / 11 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g / 12 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g / 4 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sugar (added)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g (0 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 g (0 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g (0 g)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultra-filtered milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultra-filtered milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk protein concentrate, calcium caseinate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price per shake (Amazon 12-pack)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.75</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.23</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.23</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.68</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.69</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.86</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick Premier if sugar and carbs come first. Pick Muscle Milk if price and sweeteners do.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pick Premier Protein if you want the most protein per calorie, the fewest carbs or a vitamin-fortified breakfast shake. Pick Muscle Milk if you want the cheapest protein per gram, a stevia-sweetened shake with about a dozen ingredients, or the 33 g Pro carton for more protein in the same size. Pick the Muscle Milk Pro Series powder if you need NSF Certified for Sport.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Both shakes are milk-based, both contain milk, and neither is lactose-free, so the deciding questions are the ones in the sections below: protein per carton, sugar and carbs, sweetener, lactose and cost. If you want the wider field of bottled shakes, including Fairlife Core Power and plant-based options, our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink guide</a> ranks them all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Muscle Milk and Premier Protein Products Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            One card per like-for-like product: the standard Muscle Milk shake, the Premier 30 g shake, the higher-protein Muscle Milk Pro carton, and each brand's main powder. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 16 November 2026 and change often, so treat the cost per 25 g as a snapshot. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-changed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Changed in Muscle Milk Shakes in 2026?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Muscle Milk reformulated its whole shake line in spring 2026, moving to ultra-filtered milk and removing artificial sweeteners, artificial flavors and added colors. PepsiCo announced the change in a <a href="https://www.pepsico.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026/muscle-milk-reformulates-protein-shakes-no-artificial-sweeteners-flavors-or-added-colors" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">15 May 2026 press release</a>, which gives 26 g of protein for the standard shake and 33 to 42 g for the Pro line. Any comparison written before then is comparing Premier with a shake you can no longer reliably buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The old line had two main shakes: Muscle Milk Genuine at about 25 g of protein and the Pro Series shake at about 40 g. Trade coverage in <a href="https://www.foodnavigator.com/Article/2026/06/03/muscle-milk-undergoes-its-biggest-transformation-with-pro-lineup/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">FoodNavigator</a> confirms the previous formulas carried 25 g or 40 g, and the new ones carry 26 g or 42 g. The standard shake has effectively replaced Genuine, and Muscle Milk Pro, sold as an 11 fl oz carton with 33 g and a 14 fl oz bottle with 42 g, has replaced the Pro Series shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The recipe is very different, not just the protein number. The new chocolate shake lists ultra-filtered milk first, then less than 2 percent of ascorbic acid, carrageenan, cellulose gel and gum, cocoa, natural flavor, stevia, salt, sodium citrate and vitamins A and D3, per the retailer label we checked. That is about 13 ingredients. Muscle Milk says the new formula uses fewer ingredients than the old one, and the old shakes were built on milk protein concentrates with artificial sweeteners, which is exactly what this change removed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical warnings follow. First, older Amazon listings for the Genuine and Pro Series shakes are still live, often from third-party sellers at higher prices, so check that the title says ultra-filtered milk and 26 g, 33 g or 42 g before you buy. Second, the change applies to the shakes only. Muscle Milk's Genuine and Pro Series powders are still on the old formula, with artificial sweeteners, which matters when we compare the powders later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier Protein did not reformulate its core shake. The 30 g carton still lists milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate, with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, per the Premier label at the time of writing. Premier did add a bigger shake, the Ultimate 42 g in a 14 oz bottle, which is the natural rival to the 42 g Muscle Milk Pro bottle. We cover that pair in the protein and price sections.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Has More Protein, Muscle Milk or Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier has more protein than the standard Muscle Milk shake, but less than Muscle Milk Pro. A Premier carton gives 30 g of protein, the standard Muscle Milk carton gives 26 g and the Muscle Milk Pro carton gives 33 g, per the labels at the time of writing. In the 14 oz size, the two brands tie: Premier Ultimate and Muscle Milk Pro both list 42 g of protein per bottle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per calorie tells a cleaner story than protein per carton. Protein carries 4 calories per gram, so 30 g of protein in a 160-calorie Premier carton means about 75 percent of its calories come from protein. The standard Muscle Milk shake, at 26 g in 160 calories, is about 65 percent protein, and Muscle Milk Pro, at 33 g in about 200 calories, is about 66 percent. In the big bottles, Premier Ultimate is about 76 percent protein at 220 calories, and Muscle Milk Pro 42 g is about 65 percent at 250 to 260 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands use complete dairy protein, so the quality is effectively the same. Premier's milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate are mostly casein with some whey, the same roughly 80 to 20 split found in milk. Muscle Milk's ultra-filtered milk is milk with water and some lactose filtered out, so its protein is also the natural casein and whey mix of milk. Muscle Milk describes it as a complete protein with all nine essential amino acids, which is true of any dairy protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people, the 4 g gap between Premier and standard Muscle Milk does not matter. Research on muscle building, including a <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28698222/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">2018 meta-analysis in the British Journal of Sports Medicine</a>, ties gains to total daily protein, with benefits levelling off around 1.6 g per kg of body weight per day, rather than to the exact dose in one shake. Around 20 to 40 g in a sitting covers most adults, and every shake here sits in that range. If you want to work out your own target, our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> does the arithmetic by body weight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about the 42 g bottles?</h3>

          <p>
            Premier Ultimate 42 g and Muscle Milk Pro 42 g give the same protein, but Premier does it in fewer calories and carbs. Per the labels at the time of writing, the Premier Ultimate chocolate bottle lists 220 calories, 4 g of fat, 5 g of carbohydrate and 2 g of sugar, while the Muscle Milk Pro chocolate bottle lists 250 to 260 calories, 2.5 g of fat, 16 g of carbohydrate and 4 to 5 g of sugar. The big bottles suit larger lifters or people using a shake as a meal, but for most people a 26 to 33 g carton is enough.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories-sugar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has Fewer Calories, Less Sugar and Less Fat?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier has less sugar and far fewer carbs, while the standard Muscle Milk shake has less fat; both have 160 calories. Per the labels at the time of writing, a Premier carton lists 1 g of sugar, 4 g of carbohydrate and 3 g of fat, and a standard Muscle Milk carton lists 3 g of sugar, 11 g of carbohydrate and 1.5 g of fat. Neither shake has any added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sugar gap comes from the milk itself. Muscle Milk's 3 g of sugar is milk sugar, lactose, left over after ultra-filtration, which is why the label shows 0 g of added sugar. Premier's protein is made from concentrate and caseinate that have had most of their lactose removed before the shake is mixed, so its sugar falls to 1 g. A difference of 2 g of sugar is small in a day's diet, but it can matter if you are tracking carbs tightly or managing blood sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The carb gap is larger than the sugar gap. Muscle Milk's 11 g of carbohydrate includes 2 g of fiber and 3 g of sugar, and the rest comes mainly from the milk solids and the cellulose and carrageenan thickeners. Premier's 4 g includes 2 g of fiber and 1 g of sugar. For a keto or very low-carb diet, Premier fits more easily; for everyone else, 11 g of carbs in a 26 g protein shake is still modest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fat is low in both. Muscle Milk's 1.5 g and Premier's 3 g are small amounts, with 1 g of saturated fat in each, per the labels. Sodium is close too: about 240 mg in the standard Muscle Milk carton and about 230 mg in the Premier carton we checked, though Premier's sodium varies by flavor and retailer listing. If you drink two or three shakes a day, the sodium adds up to roughly a quarter of the daily value either way.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per carton, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sugar, Premier 30 g</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sugar, Muscle Milk 26 g</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbs, Premier</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">11 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbs, Muscle Milk</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            On a cut, Premier is the slightly better tool because more of its 160 calories are protein. The difference is about 4 g of protein and 7 g of carbs per carton, which is real but not dramatic, so if you prefer Muscle Milk's taste or sweetener, it still works in a calorie deficit. People planning a large deficit, or managing diabetes, should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian about how a shake fits their plan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Uses Artificial Sweeteners, Muscle Milk or Premier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein shakes use artificial sweeteners and the 2026 Muscle Milk shakes do not. The Premier 30 g and Ultimate 42 g shakes are sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, per the Premier label at the time of writing. The reformulated Muscle Milk and Muscle Milk Pro shakes are sweetened with purified stevia leaf extract and stevia extract, per the retailer labels we checked, and Muscle Milk says they contain no artificial sweeteners, flavors or added colors.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many buyers this is the single biggest difference between the two brands. Sucralose and acesulfame potassium are approved for use in food in the United States and are used in a huge range of diet drinks and protein products. Some people avoid them anyway, for taste or out of preference, and until 2026 that ruled out both of these shakes. Now it rules out only Premier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stevia is not automatically better tasting. Buyers of stevia-sweetened drinks often report a slightly bitter or licorice-like aftertaste that sucralose does not leave, so anyone who loved the old Genuine taste should buy a small pack of the new shake before committing to a case. Premier's formula has not changed, so its taste is a known quantity. If you are choosing a Premier flavor, our <a href="/best-premier-protein-flavor/">best Premier Protein flavor guide</a> summarizes what buyers prefer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavorings differ too. Premier lists natural and artificial flavors, while Muscle Milk lists natural flavor only. Both shakes contain carrageenan as a thickener, so if you avoid carrageenan, neither one solves that. Premier also lists a small amount of sunflower or soybean oil and carries a milk and soy allergen statement, while Muscle Milk's allergen statement lists milk only.
          </p>

          <p>
            The powders are a different story. Muscle Milk did not change its Genuine or Pro Series powders, which still list sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and Premier's powder uses the same pair. So if artificial sweeteners are your dealbreaker, the Muscle Milk shake is the only product in this comparison that clears it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lactose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Better for Lactose Intolerance?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein is likely the lower-lactose shake, though neither brand publishes a lactose figure or calls its shakes lactose-free. Lactose is a sugar, so it is counted in total sugars on the nutrition label. Premier lists 1 g of total sugar and no added sugar, which means a carton cannot contain more than 1 g of lactose. The standard Muscle Milk shake lists 3 g of total sugar with no added sugar, so its lactose ceiling is about 3 g, and Muscle Milk Pro's is about 3 to 5 g depending on size and flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both numbers are low. A cup of regular milk carries about 12 g of lactose, and many people with lactose intolerance can handle a few grams at a time without symptoms. Muscle Milk's ultra-filtration removes much of the milk's lactose, which is why the brand and its trade coverage describe the new shakes as reduced in lactose. Premier gets lower still because its concentrate and caseinate are separated from the milk sugar before the shake is made.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tolerance is individual, so treat these as ceilings, not guarantees. If one carton of Muscle Milk bothers you, Premier is the logical next step. If Premier still bothers you, the problem may not be lactose at all, and carrageenan, sweeteners or simply drinking a shake fast on an empty stomach are common culprits. Our guide to <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> covers powders labelled lactose-free if you need a stricter option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither brand is suitable for a milk allergy. Both shakes are made entirely from milk proteins, and both carry a milk allergen statement; Premier adds soy. A milk allergy is an immune reaction to the protein, not the sugar, so low lactose does not help. Anyone with a diagnosed allergy should choose a plant-based shake and check with their doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the powders, the logic flips slightly. Premier's powder lists whey protein isolate first, which is filtered to remove most lactose, while the Muscle Milk Pro Series and Genuine powders use a milk protein blend that includes concentrates and caseinates. So on both shakes and powders, Premier is the safer first try for a sensitive stomach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Is Cheaper per Shake and per 25 g of Protein?
