        <div id="powders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Muscle Milk vs Premier Protein Powder: Which Tub Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Muscle Milk Pro Series is the better-value powder and the only certified one, while Premier's powder is leaner and lower in lactose. The Pro Series listing gives 50 g of protein per two-scoop serving and 11 servings in the 2 lb tub; at $27.97 that is about $1.27 per 25 g. Premier's vanilla powder gives 30 g of protein for about 150 calories per two scoops, per the label, with 17 servings; at $29.99 that is about $1.47 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification is the clearest split. Muscle Milk's Pro Series and Genuine powders are listed as NSF Certified for Sport on Amazon and by the brand, which means the program screens for substances banned in sport and checks the label claim. We could not find an NSF, Informed Choice or Informed Sport listing for any Premier Protein powder or shake, and we found no such claim for the new Muscle Milk shakes either. Drug-tested athletes should look up the exact product and flavor in the <a href="https://www.nsfsport.com/certified-products/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">NSF Certified for Sport database</a> before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Muscle Milk Genuine powder is a different animal. Its listing gives 32 g of protein per serving, but our <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk protein powder review</a> found it carries roughly 270 to 280 calories per serving with maltodextrin, creamer and oils, so it behaves more like a light mass gainer. The 4.94 lb tub cost $60.99 when we checked, about $1.49 per 25 g. It suits people trying to gain weight, not people comparing it with a lean Premier scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier's powder is the leaner everyday whey. It lists whey protein isolate first, then concentrate, at about 1 g of sugar per serving, and our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> covers its flavors and the caffeinated Cafe Latte tub. For a site-generated head-to-head on label score and price, see the <a href="/compare/muscle-milk-genuine-protein-powder-vs-premier-protein-powder/">Muscle Milk Genuine vs Premier Protein powder comparison</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powder beats bottles on price for both brands. Even the more expensive powder here costs less per 25 g than the cheapest shake, because you are not paying for the carton, the liquid and the shipping weight. If you drink a shake every day, a tub of either brand at home plus a carton in the bag for busy days is the cheapest way to use them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Is Muscle Milk or Premier Better for Weight Loss, Muscle Gain or Older Adults?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier is slightly better for weight loss, Muscle Milk Pro is slightly better value for muscle gain, and Premier's added vitamins make it the more common pick for older adults who use a shake as a light meal. None of these margins are large, and both brands do every job adequately. The choice usually comes down to sweetener, sugar and price rather than goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For weight loss, the ratio of protein to calories matters most, because protein helps with fullness and with keeping muscle in a calorie deficit. Premier's 30 g in 160 calories is the best ratio among the cartons, with only 4 g of carbs. The standard Muscle Milk shake gives 26 g for the same calories, so you get a little less protein for each calorie. If you dislike artificial sweeteners, Muscle Milk is still a perfectly good diet shake, and our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">meal replacement shake guide</a> covers options with more fiber and fat if you are swapping out a full meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle gain, total protein and cost per gram matter most. Muscle Milk Pro's 33 g carton costs about the same as a Premier carton and delivers 3 g more protein, and the Pro Series powder is the cheapest protein of either brand at about $1.27 per 25 g. Pair either shake with enough total daily protein and progressive training; the shake itself is a convenience, not a muscle builder on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            For older adults, Premier's 24 added vitamins and minerals give it an edge as a breakfast or snack shake, while Muscle Milk adds only calcium and vitamins A and D. Muscle Milk's new label is simpler and its 770 mg of calcium per carton is high, which some readers will prefer. For a broader look at shakes aimed at nutrition rather than training, see our <a href="/premier-protein-vs-ensure/">Premier Protein vs Ensure comparison</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            One health note applies to both. High-protein shakes are not right for everyone: people with kidney disease, those who are pregnant or breastfeeding, anyone on a protein-restricted or fluid-restricted diet, and older adults with medical conditions should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before drinking one or more a day. This page compares labels and prices; it is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing against the old Muscle Milk formula</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many reviews still describe Muscle Milk Genuine at 25 g with sucralose. Since spring 2026 the standard shake is 26 g of protein from ultra-filtered milk, sweetened with stevia. Check the carton or listing for ultra-filtered milk and 26 g, 33 g or 42 g so you know which formula you are actually buying or reading about.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying old-stock listings at inflated prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old Genuine and Pro Series shake listings are still live on Amazon, sometimes from third-party sellers at $50 to $60 a 12-pack. That is more than double the new shake's price per gram. Sort by the current titles, check the seller and run the cost per 25 g before adding anything to your cart.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing per carton instead of per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $1.75 carton and a $2.23 carton look very different, but they hold 26 g and 30 g of protein. Divide the pack price by total grams of protein and multiply by 25. On that basis the standard Muscle Milk shake and Muscle Milk Pro cost almost the same per gram.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming low sugar means lactose-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neither brand calls its shakes lactose-free or prints a lactose figure. Lactose sits inside total sugars, so Premier's 1 g and Muscle Milk's 3 g are ceilings, not zero. If you are sensitive, start with half a carton, and if you have a milk allergy avoid both, because the allergy is to the protein itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the powders like the shakes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Muscle Milk changed its shakes, not its powders. The Pro Series and Genuine tubs still use artificial sweeteners and a milk protein blend, and Genuine powder carries far more calories than the new shake. Premier's powder is whey, not the milk protein in its cartons. Read each label on its own terms.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

